# Property based access policies · geldata gel · Discussion #5447
created: 2023-05-09, last updated: 2026-04-16, [Read more](https://github.com/geldata/gel/discussions/5447)

Allow specifying the properties on which an access policy applies.
This would be applicable to `select`, `insert` and `update`.
### Example
```
type User {
required property name -> str;
multi link friends -> User;
access policy friends_can_select_name
allow select { name }
using (global current_user_id in .friends.id);
}
```
...
Can be achieved using postgres `WITH CHECK` to ensure only the allowed fields are updated
0 replies

# v2.0 - Changelog | Resources
created: 2022-07-28, last updated: 2026-04-29, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/resources/changelog/2_x)

These are contextual variables that are provided by the client and can be referenced in your queries and schema.
...
result = client.query("""
...
Object types can now be augmented with object-level access policies.
When
combined with global variables, access policies can be used to
push authorization logic into the database.
Copy
```
global current_user -> uuid;
type User {
required property email -> str { constraint exclusive; };
}
type BlogPost {
required property title -> str;
link author -> User;
access policy own_posts allow all using (
.author.id ?= global current_user
)
}
```
…
```
type BlogPost {
property title -> str;
}
type Person {
multi link posts -> BlogPost {
on source delete delete target;
}
}
```
Under this policy, deleting a `User` will unconditionally delete its
`posts` as well.
To avoid deleting a `Post` that is linked to by other schema entities,
append `if orphan`.
Copy
```
type Person {
multi link posts -> BlogPost {
on source delete delete target;
on source delete delete target if orphan;
}
}
```

# v4.0 - Changelog | Resources
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-03-12, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/resources/changelog/4_x)

When a user signs up, we create a new object of type `ext::auth::Identity`,
which you can link to in your own schema.
We then provide you with a token that
can be set as the global `ext::auth::client_token` which will automatically
populate another computed global called `ext::auth::ClientTokenIdentity`
which you can use directly in your access policies, or in your own globals.
...
);
type Customer {
required text: str;
required identity: ext::auth::Identity;
}
type Item {
required sku: str;
required description: str;
}
type Cart {
required customer: Customer;
multi items: Item {
quantity: int32;
};
access policy customer_has_full_access
allow all
using (global current_customer ?= .customer);
}
}
...
It can be useful to be able to create, update or delete different objects
based on some condition:
Copy
```
with
name := <str>$0,
admin := <bool>$1
select if admin then (
insert AdminUser { name := name }
) else (
insert User { name := name }
)
```

# Allows access for any entity
created: 2026-06-22, last updated: 2026-07-12, [Read more](https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/policies/policy-examples.html)

This following example shows how you might create a policy that allows the user `alice` to view the photo `VacationPhoto94.jpg`.
```
permit(
principal == User::"alice",
action == Action::"view",
resource == Photo::"VacationPhoto94.jpg"
);
```
...
permit(
principal in Group::"alice_friends",
action == Action::"view",
resource == Photo::"VacationPhoto94.jpg"
);
```
...
```
permit(
principal == User::"alice",
action in [Action::"view", Action::"edit", Action::"delete"],
resource in Album::"alice_vacation"
);
```
...
permit(
principal,
action in [Action::"listPhotos", Action::"view"],
resource in Album::"device_prototypes"
)
when {
principal.department == "HardwareEngineering" &&
principal.jobLevel >= 5
};
...
This following example shows how you might create a policy that allows all principals to perform any action on resources for which they have `owner` attribute.
```
permit(
principal,
action,
resource
)
when {
principal == resource.owner
};

# Bringing .NET to EdgeDB | Gel Blog
created: 2022-11-01, last updated: 2026-02-21, [Read more](https://www.geldata.com/blog/bringing-net-to-edgedb)

```
using EdgeDB.State;
var configuredClient = client
.WithConfig(conf =>
{
conf.AllowDMLInFunctions = true;
conf.ApplyAccessPolicies = true;
conf.DDLPolicy = DDLPolicy.AlwaysAllow;
conf.QueryExecutionTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
conf.IdleTransationTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
})
```
```
open EdgeDB.State
let configuredClient = client.WithConfig(fun conf ->
conf.AllowDMLInFunctions <- true
conf.ApplyAccessPolicies <- true
conf.DDLPolicy <- DDLPolicy.AlwaysAllow
conf.QueryExecutionTimeout <- TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)
conf.IdleTransationTimeout <- TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)
```

